<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Quick Look app extensions must be sandboxed regardless of whether the
      host app is (the main CinePlayer app deliberately is not — see
      CinePlayerApp/CinePlayer.entitlements). This is NOT a copy of that
      file: the host app's entitlements are a debug-only
      `get-task-allow` and nothing else (it isn't sandboxed at all), which
      would be meaningless/wrong here.

      **Correction, 2026-08-13 — the previous version of this comment's
      claim was wrong; keep the file-access entitlements below.** It had
      claimed no file-access entitlement was needed, based on one
      open-source reference project. Directly inspecting this machine's own
      `lsregister -dump` output for every *currently installed, working*
      `com.apple.quicklook.thumbnail` provider (Apple's own
      HydraQLThumbnailExtension, SceneKitQLThumbnailExtension, etc. —
      genuinely shipping, real examples on this exact macOS/Xcode version,
      a stronger source than a single third-party sample) shows every one
      of them declaring both entitlements below alongside app-sandbox. Not
      proven to be the fix for this project's specific qlmanage -t hang
      (see the `ThumbnailProvider.swift` investigation notes: that hang was
      independently root-caused to a genuine environment/session-level
      limitation of the com.apple.quicklook.thumbnail extension point's
      dispatch mechanism, reproducing identically for Apple's own
      HydraQLThumbnailExtension against an unrelated control file even with
      these same entitlements already present — so this alone won't make
      qlmanage succeed in that same constrained environment). Kept anyway
      as a real, evidence-based parity fix with every working reference
      example actually found on this system, and worth having regardless
      once run somewhere the extension point's dispatch itself works.

      Referenced directly via the CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS build setting in
      project.yml (not xcodegen's own `entitlements:` target property,
      which — confirmed the hard way — regenerates/overwrites this file
      from a `properties:` map on every `xcodegen generate` and would blank
      it out entirely if that map were left empty). This matches how the
      main CinePlayer target's own CinePlayer.entitlements is already
      referenced.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-only</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.downloads.read-only</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
